## Why This Matters

**Compressed Progression**

80 levels takes months. 20 levels takes hours.

Roguelike gameplay demands:
- Short sessions (1-2 hours)
- Quick progression milestones
- Replayability (multiple characters)

20-level cap enables all three.

**Dual-Specialization Gameplay**

60 talent points enables filling 2 trees:
- Paladin: Holy (healing) + Protection (tanking)
- Mage: Frost (control) + Fire (damage)
- Druid: Restoration (healing) + Feral (DPS/tank)

This creates **versatile characters** who can fulfill multiple roles. Critical for solo/small-group play.

40 points (2 per level) was tested and rejected—too restrictive for dual-spec.

**Meaningful Choices**

With ~80-120 options in tiers 1-3 and only 60 points:
- Can't max everything
- Must choose specializations
- Builds feel distinct

If you could max all trees, choices don't matter. 60 points forces trade-offs.

**Immediate Customization**

First talent point at 33% of level 1 XP (minutes into the game):
- No waiting until level 10 (hours into the game)
- Immediate character differentiation
- Players engage with talent system early

This sets expectations: "This game is about builds."

